said it was almost over I literally went &quot;What? But it just started?&quot;, sure enough, reality did not agree with me. I'd been listening for almost an hour. So, thanks for that very entertaining hour and the great ideas for new shows to watch! Also, Buffy FOREVER.<br /> <br /> Now, to answer your question near the end, when you asked us to post ideas for topics. Here are my thoughts.<br /> <br /> 1 - Thought experiments (Design Focus)<br /> May be it's just me but I love hearing about your processes, how you approach design problems and what are your core design philosophies. As an extention to that, I think it might be interesting to do some thought experiments focus on game design. For example, if you were free of any concerns for page count, budget and marketability, what would your dream game look like? What next-big evolution to how we approach gaming, tell stories or design games would you like to see happen or usher in yourselves? How <i>would</i> you adapt a completely seemingly incompatible type of game to tabletop? For example, Eddy mentioned Castlevania - more of an exploration focused game - in a previous episode, how exactly would each of you tackle adapting this particular puzzle? Speaking of puzzle, in particular I would be interested in how would adapt something like Myst. I know it's already been adapted, but, how would <i>you</i> do it? For example, amazing as the adaptation is, it seemed to ignore the puzzle-heavy element of the video games, is this something you would have done or would you have found a way to integrate them as a core mechanic by giving DMs the tools to create some easily? If so, how would you have done it?<br /> <br /> 2 - Inheritence<br /> In episode 19 with Phil, there was a very interesting question near the end. You asked, if he <i>hadn't</i> inherited mage. If he had been able to build it himself, from the ground up, how different would it have been? That was a brilliant question and a fascinating topic that could be reused. How would Justin have done Vampire if he'd been in charge from day one? How would Ethan have shaped Werewolf? What about the current generation of developers working on the 20th editions and the 5th editions, what would they have done differently if they had been in charge 20+ years ago instead of just now?<br /> <br /> Similarly, how has those games influenced your approach to later games. For example, for Ethan, one can see a lot of parallels between Werewolf the Apocalypse and Changeling: the Lost, in terms of gifts/contracts and the concepts/spirits that grant them. In what other ways has years of working on Vampire, Werewolf, Mages, etc. shaped the way you designed your next game where you <i>were</i> in charge from day one.<br /> <br /> Hope this is of some use to you guys and thanks again for a consistently entertaining podcast.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3822-yiodan","name":"Yiodan","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}